From 268ad914f66119bd6cb10ad6522351b999e98a70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AkiyamaRin <akir1n@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 00:09:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] perf: cut search and update tail latency

Search
- diversifyRankedChunks now maintains a running redundancy maximum instead of
  recomputing similarity against every already-selected candidate on each step:
  O(n^3) -> O(n^2) similarity evaluations (42.5M -> 201k at n=634), identical
  output because only Math.max accumulation order is exploited.
- Bound the diversity window to 256 candidates after ranking/filtering; the tail
  keeps its score order and stays in the result set, so total_count/has_more are
  unchanged.
- Resolve the query embedding once per request and thread it through KB iteration
  and auto-mode retries instead of re-embedding per KB (5 KBs = 5 embeddings).
- searchVectorFile reports vector/chunk counts; a sidecar whose length no longer
  matches the KB's chunks is skipped with a warning instead of being paired
  positionally (which silently attaches vectors to the wrong chunks).
- Index chunks by (kb_id, file_path, start_line); adaptive context expansion was
  scanning every row of the KB per result.

Update
- Skip embedding-input measurement for chunks whose content_hash is already
  stored and whose embedding-input policy is current. Measurement is a per-chunk
  worker round trip (~5-10ms, no batching win) and was running before the reuse
  check, so an unchanged 6k-chunk KB spent ~60s measuring nothing.
- When nothing changed and every stored vector is still readable, skip the vector
  sidecar rewrite and the symbol rebuild. Readability is verified by reading, so
  the missing-vector repair path still works.
- Claim the KB with a conditional UPDATE and a cross-process lock file; a second
  writer now fails with a clear message instead of interleaving.
